  <meta property="og:description" content="A practical checklist for moving to Shopify without creating unnecessary problems." />
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://swishtag.com/insights/blog/" />
  <script slot="head" is:inline type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Shopify Migration Checklist: 12 Steps for 2026","datePublished":"2026-08-03","dateModified":"2026-08-03","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Swishtag"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Swishtag"},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://swishtag.com/insights/blog/"}</script>
  <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <main>
    <section class="container article-hero">
      <div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/../insights/">Insights</a><span>/</span><a href="/../insights/">Migration</a><span>/</span><span>Shopify Migration Checklist</span></div>
      <div class="article-hero-main"><div class="eyebrow">SHOPIFY MIGRATION</div><h1>Shopify Migration Checklist: 12 Steps for 2026</h1><p class="article-dek">Moving to Shopify involves more than transferring products. This guide walks through the decisions, data and technical work that should happen before, during and after migration.</p><div class="hero-meta"><span>Swishtag Team</span><span>August 3, 2026</span><span>8 min read</span></div></div>
    </section>

    <div class="featured-article-image is-loading" role="img" aria-label="Loading featured article image" aria-busy="true">
      <div class="insights-image-placeholder insights-image-placeholder--loading" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>

    <section class="container article-layout">
      <aside class="share-rail" aria-label="Share this article">
        <!-- <button class="share-btn" aria-label="Copy link">↗</button> -->
        <button class="share-btn" aria-label="Share on LinkedIn"> <a    href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/helloswishtag/posts/" target="_blank"  >in</a>  </button>
        <!-- <button class="share-btn" aria-label="Share by email">@</button> -->
      </aside>
      <article class="article-body">
        <details class="toc-mobile"><summary>In this insight</summary><div><a href="#why">Why migrations go wrong</a><a href="#audit">1. Audit your current system</a><a href="#data">2. Define your data model</a><a href="#seo">3. Protect SEO</a><a href="#integrations">4. Map integrations</a><a href="#qa">5. Test before launch</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></div></details>

        <p>A Shopify migration is rarely just a website rebuild. The storefront may be the visible part, but the risky work usually sits underneath it: product data, customer accounts, order history, redirects, payment configuration, ERP connections, custom pricing and internal workflows.</p>
        <p>The safest way to migrate is to treat the project as a business-system change. Design still matters, but architecture and data decisions need to happen first.</p>

        <div class="takeaway"><strong>The takeaway</strong>A Shopify migration should be treated as a business-system migration, not simply a theme redesign.</div>

        <h2 id="why">Why Shopify migrations go wrong</h2>
        <p>Most failed migrations are not caused by Shopify itself. They happen when teams underestimate the number of dependencies around the existing store. A redirect gets missed. An app replaces one feature but breaks another. A B2B customer loses account-specific pricing. A warehouse workflow no longer receives the data it expects.</p>
        <p>The fix is simple in principle: map the operating system before you move the storefront.</p>

        <div class="quote">“Move the system deliberately, not page by page.”</div>

        <h2 id="audit">1. Audit your current commerce system</h2>
        <p>Before choosing a theme, document what your current ecommerce setup actually does. That includes visible features and behind-the-scenes processes.</p>
        <div class="step-list">
          <div class="step"><div><h3>Catalog</h3><p>Products, variants, metafields, bundles, catalogs, price lists and custom attributes.</p></div></div>
          <div class="step"><div><h3>Customers</h3><p>Accounts, permissions, B2B companies, locations, tags and customer-specific rules.</p></div></div>
          <div class="step"><div><h3>Operations</h3><p>Order routing, fulfillment, approvals, supplier handoffs and accounting connections.</p></div></div>
          <div class="step"><div><h3>Marketing</h3><p>SEO pages, analytics, email flows, feeds, tracking pixels and campaign landing pages.</p></div></div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="data">2. Define the Shopify data model before importing anything</h2>
        <p>Do not automatically reproduce the old database structure inside Shopify. Some legacy platforms accumulate fields and workarounds that no longer serve a purpose. Decide what belongs in standard Shopify objects, what should become metafields, and what belongs in an app or external system.</p>
        <h3>Clean before you migrate</h3>
        <ul><li>Remove duplicate products and customers.</li><li>Standardize SKUs and option names.</li><li>Decide which historical orders are genuinely useful.</li><li>Map legacy URLs before changing page structure.</li></ul>

        <div class="inline-cta"><h3>Planning a Shopify migration?</h3><p>Swishtag can help map your existing ecommerce system, identify migration risks and build the new Shopify architecture before anything moves.</p><a class="btn" href="/../book-demo/">Talk to a Shopify expert</a></div>

        <h2 id="seo">3. Protect SEO before the new store launches</h2>
        <p>SEO protection starts before development is complete. Export existing URLs, rankings and organic landing pages, then create a redirect map that preserves high-value paths wherever possible.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Before launch</th><th>After launch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>URLs</td><td>Export and map</td><td>Check redirects</td></tr><tr><td>Metadata</td><td>Preserve priority pages</td><td>Validate indexing</td></tr><tr><td>Analytics</td><td>Configure tracking</td><td>Compare traffic</td></tr><tr><td>Internal links</td><td>Update templates</td><td>Crawl for errors</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <h2 id="integrations">4. Map integrations and operational dependencies</h2>
        <p>List every system that currently sends data to, or receives data from, your store. This may include ERP, accounting, fulfillment, suppliers, PIM, CRM, shipping, search, reviews and marketing platforms.</p>
        <p>The right question is not “Is there a Shopify app?” The better question is “What data needs to move, when does it need to move, and which system should own it?”</p>

        <h2 id="qa">5. Test the migration like an operating system</h2>
        <p>Run QA against the actual workflows your team depends on. Product browsing and checkout are only the beginning.</p>
        <ol><li>Test representative product types and customer groups.</li><li>Place test orders using real shipping and tax scenarios.</li><li>Confirm downstream systems receive the right data.</li><li>Validate refunds, cancellations and fulfillment updates.</li><li>Crawl redirects and priority SEO pages.</li><li>Test mobile performance on real devices.</li></ol>

        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Do not launch because the homepage looks finished.</strong>Launch when the important customer and operational workflows have been tested end to end.</div>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <div class="faq-item"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">How long does a Shopify migration take?<span>+</span></button><div class="faq-answer">It depends on catalog complexity, integrations, custom logic and the amount of data being moved. A small store can move quickly, while a B2B or integration-heavy migration needs a staged plan.</div></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Should every historical order be migrated?<span>+</span></button><div class="faq-answer">Not always. The decision should be based on customer-service needs, reporting requirements and whether the historical data is useful inside Shopify.</div></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">Can SEO rankings be preserved?<span>+</span></button><div class="faq-answer">You can reduce SEO risk significantly with careful URL mapping, redirects, metadata preservation, crawl testing and post-launch monitoring.</div></div>
        </div>

        <div class="article-end"><div class="meta"><span>Updated August 3, 2026</span><span>Share this insight</span></div><div class="author-card"><div class="author-avatar">S</div><div><h4>Swishtag Team</h4><p>Commerce strategists, Shopify developers and automation specialists sharing practical lessons from building real ecommerce systems.</p></div></div></div>
      </article>

